  <p>
    "Pigmentation" covers several different conditions that look broadly similar and respond
    completely differently. Establishing which one you have is most of the work, and it is the
    reason the same cream helps one person and does nothing for another.
  </p>

  <h2>The common types</h2>

  <h3>Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation</h3>
  <p>
    Brown marks left where a spot, rash, cut or burn healed. Very common in Indian skin, because
    deeper skin tones produce more pigment in response to inflammation. It fades on its own over
    months, faster with sun protection and the right topical treatment — and faster still if
    whatever caused the inflammation is being treated.
  </p>

  <h3>Melasma</h3>
  <p>
    Symmetrical brown patching across the cheeks, forehead and upper lip, driven by sun, visible
    light and hormones. Chronic and relapsing.
    <a href="/conditions/melasma/">Covered in detail on its own page.</a>
  </p>

  <h3>Sun damage and tanning</h3>
  <p>
    Diffuse darkening and dullness on exposed skin, with freckling and scattered spots over years.
    The most preventable type and the most responsive.
  </p>

  <h3>Periorbital hyperpigmentation — dark circles</h3>
  <p>
    Often assumed to be pigment when it is actually shadow from under-eye hollowing, or visible
    vessels through thin skin, or a combination. Treating the wrong component is why so many
    under-eye creams disappoint.
  </p>

  <h3>Acanthosis nigricans</h3>
  <p>
    Velvety dark thickening in the neck, armpits and groin. It is a sign of insulin resistance,
    often with PCOS or weight gain. Creams make a marginal difference; addressing the metabolic
    cause makes a real one. This is worth investigating rather than bleaching.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    The pattern, the distribution and the history usually identify the type. Dermoscopy helps
    judge whether pigment sits superficially or deeper, which predicts how well and how fast it
    will respond. Every cream, cosmetic and hair product in use is reviewed, because pigmented
    contact dermatitis looks like melasma and will not clear while the trigger continues.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where acanthosis nigricans is present, blood sugar and insulin resistance are assessed, and
    hormonal testing is added where PCOS is suspected.
  </p>

  <h2>What works</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sun protection</strong> — broad-spectrum, daily, reapplied. Tinted formulations containing iron oxide also block visible light, which matters in deeper skin</li>
    <li><strong>Topical actives</strong> — retinoids, azelaic acid, vitamin C, kojic acid, niacinamide, and hydroquinone in supervised courses</li>
    <li><strong>Chemical peels</strong> — useful for superficial pigment, at conservative strengths in Indian skin</li>
    <li><strong>Laser toning</strong> — selectively, at careful settings, and usually after topical treatment is established</li>
    <li><strong>Treating the cause</strong> — acne, eczema or insulin resistance, where that is what is generating the pigment</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>A caution specific to Indian skin</h3>
    <p>
      Deeper skin tones pigment in response to injury. Aggressive peels, high-energy lasers and
      harsh scrubs can therefore cause exactly the problem they were meant to treat, and the
      rebound is harder to clear than the original. Conservative settings and patience genuinely
      produce better results here.
    </p>
  </div>
